Prediction-powered inference (PPI) turns a fixed predictor into a valid confidence interval for a mean, and PPI++ is its strongest form when the labels, the unlabeled records and the predictor are given. We ask how much shorter such an interval can be without changing any of the three. We write the exact gap between PPI++ and the classical variance floor, which vanishes if and only if the regression of the outcome on the prediction is affine, and a break-even label count above which learning a calibrated control variate from the same labels pays. On the nine datasets of the \texttt{ppi-python} package, at the reference label count ($n=500$; $200$ on the smallest pool), a cross-fitted interval that chooses between PPI++ and calibrated alternatives is within $1\%$ of the width of the strongest valid PPI++ baseline on every dataset (a label saving of at most $1.1\%$), and an estimated floor is at most $3.3\%$ shorter than the package's interval. The one gain over the package, $13\%$ of the labels at $n=100$ on \texttt{gene\_expression}, comes from its clip of the tuning weight to $[0,1]$, not from calibration. With $100$ labels or fewer learning costs width (median $1.9\%$). Where the regression is far from affine (synthetic populations) savings reach $47\%$ of the labels, and the break-even formula holds where it can be tested. When labels are not missing at random, or under distribution shift, no interval is valid.
